Translated Content 29:
Soon Aluda cast his eye upon the trail of the Kists.  
He shifted swiftly; the gun went off with a crack:  
for one thief-bandit, a Ghilgveli, a bad hour had come;  
he falls from his horse, hanging head and neck downward;  
the bullet wound is in the shoulder-blade, and fire seems to cling to it;  
the slain Ghilgveli, his comrade, is dragging the trigger.  
Mutsal’s gun burst forth, the cliff face seemed to split apart,  
and the leaden bullet fell into Aluda’s skirt.

Mutsal does not wish for death,  
he does not lose the color of a wolf;  
he tears and tramples, lying in the wound,  
the mountain grass.

Aluda shot him once too;  
he does not lose the moment of his trial.  
He also threw Aluda his rifle,  
and said one more word to him:

– Now let it be yours, you dog of another faith;  
may it not fall into another’s hands.

Translated Content 0:
II. The sun has risen in the sky, the mists have descended into the ravines;  
The giants of the Caucasus have gathered at the edge of the sky.  
The hawks, the bird-chasers, have set out for the hunt;  
The eagles are being pursued by the falcons, their attendants for eating at no cost.

