Translated Content 23:
Shatili has not yet been reached by the light of the midday sun;  
they do not let the descender in, the sky is roofed by the cliff.

A youth would come, hill by hill, his head does not weigh him down;  
mist has settled on his face, black, shed from the heart.

On the throne hangs the arm of Mutsali’s brother,  
a bazala adorned with silver, that Khurasani sword.

It is set against the rocky crags, where the wind whistles;  
in summer it watches for the sound of gunfire, in winter avalanches descend.

Many times the storm has overtaken me;  
the black raven was barking.

The greedy vulture would flap above his head;  
no one could ruin his fortune, no one could do him harm.

The scorching rays of the sun melt even the hands of the infidel.  
The snake could not cut through the threshold; even today it is alive.

Sometimes a storm sets in, sometimes the sun shines bright, and it is fair weather.  
What can one do? Many a mustached dead man has been seen!

Many times the blood stream of those slain for slaughter has gone forth;  
the evil waters of Ardoti have turned red.

Translated Content 24:
Shatili has not yet been reached by the light of the midday sun;  
they do not let the one who descends pass, for the sky is covered by the cliff.  

A youth was coming, step by step, his head not proud;  
mist had settled on his face, black as if drawn from the heart.  

On the throne hangs the arm of Mutsali’s brother,  
a bazala adorned with silver, that Khorsani sword.  

It leans against the rocky crag, where hope is a stone;  
in summer it watches for the sound of a gun, in winter avalanches descend.  

Many times the storm has overtaken it;  
a black raven was cawing.  

An insatiable eagle was flapping above it;  
no one could tame fate, no one could overcome evil.  

The scorching rays of the sun are melting even the hands of the unbeliever.  
The serpent could not cut through the threshold; even today it is alive.  

Sometimes a storm sets in, sometimes the sun shines—there is fair weather.  
What have they done? Many a mustached dead man has been seen!  

Many times the stream of blood of those slain for battle has flowed away;  
the evil waters of Ardoti have turned red.

Translated Content 5:
Whoever thirsts for enmity, let him open the door of his house,  
let blood pool in the hearth, and let himself stand there too.  
Let it be drunk as wine, and used as bread is used.  
Let him make the sign of the cross, as if he were in a church.  
Let there be a wedding in blood, and there let the cross be set;  
let him invite guests, and line up the troops.  
Let a bed be laid in blood, and let a mate lie beside him;  
let him have many children, many sons and daughters;  
let him dig a grave there too, and let the dead be buried there.

