Translated Content 0:
The new clothes of the Khevsurs turn to ash,  
they look at Aluda with hostility,  
they shout at him angrily:

“Die, death would be better for you, since you are a liar;  
take off the snake-mouthed one, throw it to the women as a spindle;  
the shield — for stamping cloth, the rifle — hang it outside;  
the pistol is only fit to be spun like a spindle!  
You have run away from the Kist’s son, you have turned into a woman.  
You killed him, yet you did not cut off his right hand; why did you follow him from behind?!”

Translated Content 1:
The new clothes of the Khevsurs turn to rags,  
they look at Aluda with ill will,  
they shout at him in dry anger:  
“Die; death is better for you, since you are a liar;  
take off the serpent-mouth one,  
and throw it to the women as a spindle;  
the shield — for beating the loom’s warp;  
the rifle — hang it outside;  
the pistol will be useful for spinning, as a distaff!  
You have run away from the Kist’s son,  
you have turned into a woman.  
You killed him, yet did not cut off his right hand;  
why did you follow after him from behind?!”

Translated Content 4:
The Khevsurs’ newly sharpened knives turn into a forest,  
they look at Aluda with ill will,  
they shout at him in a dry voice:  
“Die; death would suit you better, you liar;  
take off the serpent’s face, and throw it to the women as a spindle;  
the shield is for stamping cloth, the rifle for hanging outside;  
the pistol is useful only for spinning, like a distaff!  
You have fled from the Kist’s son, you have turned into a woman.  
Kill him—why did you not strike with your right hand,  
why did you follow after him with your left?!”

