Translated Content 3:
Upon hearing this, Mindia’s eyes filled with tears;  
he can no longer help Aluda,  
he folds his arms over his chest.  
The armed sons of the Khevsurs lift their shields;  
they must confront Ketelauri,  
the bronze rings clang sharply.  
The Khevsurs, seated in a host, are overtaken by children;  
in one child’s hand are the bones of a right arm, bent at the elbow.

Translated Content 8:
Hearing this, Mindia’s eyes welled with tears;  
he can no longer help Aluda,  
he crosses his arms upon his chest.  
The sons of the Khevsurs, enraged, seize their shields;  
they must make Ketelauri know,  
the bronze rings clang fiercely.  
The Khevsurs, seated as an army, are overtaken by children;  
in one hand he has grasped the bones of a right hand, bent at the wrist.

Translated Content 9:
– Vajno, I seek for you the lord’s mercy; hide the good estate, and I will bring you the enemy’s hand.  
The raven was carrying it with its talons, steering toward the cliff’s cove.  
At that time I shot an arrow at the one proudly flying through the sky;  
the shot proved true, and it circled the raven’s wing;  
with its claw it let go of the prey, and it was flung to the ground.

Translated Content 22:
– Noble sir, I strive to win for you the lord’s favor; hide the estate from harm, and I shall bring you the enemy’s hand.  
The raven was carrying it with a claw, steering toward the cliff’s cove.  
At that moment I shot an arrow at the one proudly flying through the sky;  
the shot proved true, and it struck the raven’s wing;  
it let go of the prey with its claw, and it fell to the ground.

Translated Content 17:
Aluda says angrily:  
– Women, do not be so talkative;  
come, follow me, let us go;  
God has granted us this fate;  
do not offend the cross, do not curse the community,  
do not turn into fools!

