Translated Content 17:
They sent one spy, and quietly gave him instructions:  
let him go to Jokhola as a neighbor, as a brother;  
let him not, by any means, let the village’s intention reach his ears.  
Let him dine, let him chat, let him learn the guest’s sleeping place;  
at night let them fall upon him, bind him,  
so they need not wait long.

He went as a listener, with smooth-tongued speech;  
he congratulates Jokhola on his name-day, is cheerful, and bears no grudge.  
He jokes, tells stories, and sharpens his tongue with a whetstone.  
Who knows, his heart may be full of serpent’s poison and venom.

Translated Content 0:
The sun had not yet risen, the dew still slept on the grass,  
the breeze had not yet blown, had not yet spread below,  
countless men and women had gathered on the meadow.  
Zviadauri was being brought along, hands bound, amid the crowd;  
everyone rejoiced at his killing—who then would begin the mourning?!  
Death frightens us all; if it is another’s they are killing, we want to watch;  
men often do not feel how great a sin they are committing.  
How many evil souls I know who walk with untroubled brows;  
and who would not wish to crush the one who torments them?!

