Translated Content 9:
Evening has come; from the mountain the sunbeam has descended,  
darkness is creeping in, the light has breathed its last.  
The sunbeams have stood still, have all stood still; the sand no longer glitters;  
nowhere is it seen—the white hair of the black mountain has vanished;  
the face of that rocky cliff, embroidered and woven with sorrow,  
which is forever mourning, from it clear tears flow.  
For death, mourning is fitting; for a dead brother, a sister’s tears;  
for the forest, the flight of ox and deer; at times, the howling of the wolf.  
For a brave man, death in battle, in hand, a broken sword;  
for war, the joy of the victors and the defeat of the enemy.

