Translated Content 3:
Zviadaur was mourned by the turmoil and tossing of the water,  
by the sigh descending like a breeze from the high mountain.  
The tears of the army of clouds are commanded by God.  

And by the spring stands a woman — fair, beautiful — a Kist woman;  
she pours water on her forehead and breast, and from time to time faints.  
She wept much in silence, though from time to time she trembled;  
Zviadaur’s death appeared to her eyes.  

She was crying, yet it was not crying; she was ashamed:  
on one side, she feared the honor of the clan; on the other, God frightened her;  
the wrath of the Kist enemy would be poured upon the head of the mourner.  
This was the mind’s thought, but the heart did its own;  
the death of a man like a man would not fade from her heart.  

That image had been driven into the woman’s heart like an arrow;  
it had laid upon the beautiful one the mourning of the slain around her neck.  
She waits for darkness, to go and weep for the dead by night;  
she no longer thought how Jqola was doing.  

Whose wife is she, whose husband does she mourn?  
What is she doing, gone mad with grief?!  
Perhaps they are killing Jqola, and have broken open the doors of the house!

Translated Content 9:
Zviadaur was mourned by the turmoil and agitation of the water,  
the sigh flowing down like a breeze from the high mountain.  
The tears of the army of mists are commanded by God.  

And at the spring stands a woman — fair, beautiful — a Kist woman;  
she pours water on her forehead and breasts, and from time to time faints.  
She wept much in silence, though from time to time she shuddered;  
the death of Zviadaur appeared to her in her eyes.  

She was crying, but it was not truly crying; she was ashamed:  
on one hand, she has regard for the clan; on the other, God frightens her;  
upon the head of the mourner for the enemy of Kisteti, wrath will be poured out.  

This was the mind’s thought, but the heart was doing its own will;  
the death of a man like a man did not fade from her heart.  
That image was driven into the woman’s heart like an arrow;  
it laid upon the beautiful one the grief of the slain as a burden on her neck.  

She waits for darkness to come, to go and weep for the dead at night;  
she no longer thought how Jqjola might be in turmoil.  
Whose wife is she, whose husband does she mourn? What is she doing, out of her senses?!  
Perhaps they are killing Jqjola, and have broken open the doors of the house!

Translated Content 11:
Zviadaur was mourned by the turmoil and tossing of the water,  
by the sigh, like a breeze, that came down from the high mountain.  
The tear of the army of clouds is ordained by God.  
And at the spring stands a woman — fair, beautiful — a Kist woman;  
she pours water on her forehead and breast, and from time to time faints.  

She wept much in silence, though from time to time she shuddered;  
the death of Zviadaur appeared before her eyes.  
She was crying, yet it was not crying; she was ashamed:  
on one side, she fears the honor of the clan; on the other — God frightens her;  
the mourning for the enemy of Kisteti will pour down wrath upon her head.  

This was the mind’s thought, but the heart did its own will;  
the death of a man like a man would not fade from her heart.  
That image pierced the woman’s heart like an arrow;  
it laid upon the beautiful one the grief of the slain as a burden on her neck.  

She waits for darkness to come, to go and weep for the dead at night;  
she no longer thought how Jqola might be.  
Whose wife is she, whose husband does she mourn? What is she doing, out of her senses?!  
Perhaps they are killing Jqola, and have broken open the doors of the house too!

