Translated Content 11:
A tower is attached to the house, blackened with soot,  
day and night shaken by the firing of rifles.  
What can give rest to the poor soul,  
while enemies are all around,  
while the desire for revenge has settled in the heart of mankind,  
and many bullets fired by the enemy have struck the chest!

Translated Content 5:
In the villages there is uproar, the shouting is great:

“Let it be made known where the serpent-eater Mindia is;  
the army of the Kists is coming, they have broken the bridge over the Argun;  
the meeting of the enemy, men, now depends on us!”

Great preparations were made, the people were surging like the sea;  
whichever way you looked, rifles and swords were flashing.  
For a long time the Khevsurs had no longer been cutting down enemy detachments,  
no longer mercilessly trampling their devastated camp;  
famished for war, they could scarcely wait for morning,  
and everyone had this in their heart: who will kill the enemy leader first,  
cut off his head and right hand, and return home in glory?  
At the feast in honor of the shrine, with candles, the cup of reward will be presented.  
His name too will remain forever worthy of praise.  
In the fortress-towers the women and children were going about;  
they had prepared in advance the bundles of provisions for their husbands and sons.

Translated Content 19:
In the villages there was a great uproar, a loud clamor and shouting:

“Let it be made known where the serpent-eater Mindia is;  
the army of the Kists is coming, they have broken the bridge over the Argun;  
to meet the enemy, young men, now it depends on us!”

Great preparations were made, the people were surging like the sea;  
whichever way you looked, rifles and swords were flashing.  
For a long time the Khevsurs had not been cutting down the enemy’s detachments,  
nor had they mercilessly trampled their devastated camp;  
hungry for war, they could scarcely wait for morning,  
and everyone had this in their heart — who will kill the enemy leader first,  
cut off his head and right hand, and return home in glory,  
to be awarded the cup with candles at the shrine as a prize of valor.  
His name too will remain forever worthy of praise.  
Into the fortress-towers went the women with their children;  
they had prepared in advance the travel bags of husbands and sons.

Translated Content 3:
No fire is seen anywhere outside, no shepherd’s whistle is heard;  
no needle’s offerings were left at the door.  
In the village, all have settled down;  
the tower-forts shelter them,  
and they hide sheep and cattle,  
and hidden treasures in secret places.

Indeed I see people bringing water, shields, and swords;  
from a thousand places the fortress is breaking, and there is a house by the Kavi fortress;  
so much misery, and only a mourner is lacking;  
we thought we were not suffering, we thought we were unharmed,  
but in the end I saw that with house and home we too were cast out.

The flood came rushing from above, tore the fortress’s foundation away,  
and carried it down into the gravel bed of the Aragvi as well.  
It took the house too, from the upper story, and swept us along with it;  
I want to cry out, “Help!” but I could no longer find my voice.  
Yet I was holding the children to my chest, begging the holy icons and God for help.  
At the same time I tried to get out, edging toward the bank;  
I cover the children’s faces with the veil of a black kerchief.  
And each time I struggled to the shore, each time, alas,  
I was met by the faces of men, black as pitch.  
They pushed me with their hands, threw me back into the water, and shouted after me:  
“Where are you going? Go on, follow the water; it is God’s command!”

Translated Content 25:
Indeed, I watch how people bring water, shields, and swords;  
from a thousand places the fortress is breaking, and there is a house by the Kav fortress;  
so much misery, and only a mourner is lacking;  
we thought we were not suffering, we thought we were unharmed,  
but in the end I saw that, with house and home, we too were cast aside.

The flood came rushing from above, tore the foundation from the fortress,  
and carried it down into the gravel bed of the Aragvi as well.  
It took the house too, from the upper floor, and swept us along with it;  
I wanted to cry out, “Help me!” but I could no longer find my voice.  
Yet I held the children to my breast, and begged the holy icons and God for help.  
At the same time I tried to get out, edging toward the bank;  
I cover the children’s faces with the veil of my black kerchief.  
And every time I struggled up to the shore, each time, harshly,  
the faces of men met me there, black as pitch.  
They shoved me, threw me into the water, and shouted after me:  
“Where are you going? Go on, follow the water; it is God’s command!”

Translated Content 27:
Many a heart was troubled by a thousand kinds of fear—  
let no one be surprised, for this comes to us by nature;  
in times of danger, we remember the beloved object with fear!..

Translated Content 0:
They said this, and at that very moment turned back, ran away like thieves,  
they have drawn swords, they hold spears in their hands;  
it is hard, if we think of it, those moments of life and death; what can they do!  
if they are shamed, it is hard, they will be dressed in robes,  
headscarves will be placed over their heads, and their hats will fall off,  
if they do not exert themselves, like cowards, the wicked.

Translated Content 16:
They said this, and at that very moment they turned back,  
ran off like thieves,  
with drawn swords,  
spears held in their hands;  
it is hard, if we think of it,  
those moments of life and death;  
what can they do!  
if they are shamed, it is hard,  
jackets will be put on their bodies,  
scarves will be placed over their heads,  
and their hats will fall off,  
if they do not exert themselves,  
like cowards, the bad ones.

Translated Content 28:
The captive grinds his teeth and tries,  
he must have his hands untied;  
for a long time he struggles, rolls about,  
warmed through by the worm.  

He longs to die with his brothers,  
they are no longer whole;  
it is hard for them, and he has no army left  
to come to their aid.
